<section>
    <h2>Replay or record?</h2>
    <table class="compare" style="font-size: 0.46em;">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th style="width: 30%"><code>SYMFONY_HTTP_RECORDER</code></th>
                <th>Behaviour</th>
                <th style="width: 28%">When</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr class="fragment">
                <td><i>(empty)</i> or <code>replay</code></td>
                <td>Replays the <code>.har</code> file. No matching entry: <code>HarEntryNotFoundException</code>, <b>never the network</b></td>
                <td>Default, CI</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="fragment">
                <td><code>missing</code></td>
                <td>Replays; if the entry is missing, makes the real call and appends it to the file</td>
                <td>First recording, new call</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="fragment">
                <td><code>record</code></td>
                <td>Always the real call; rewrites the files of the tests that ran</td>
                <td>Refresh after an API change</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="fragment">
                <td><code>passthrough</code></td>
                <td>Real calls, <code>.har</code> files neither read nor written</td>
                <td>Check the tests against the real services</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="fragment callout" style="font-size: 0.6em; margin-top: 0.6em;">
        <b>Fail closed</b>: a committed test <b>never</b> touches the network. Recording is an explicit choice, made at launch.
    </p>
    <aside class="notes">
        <p>Let's recap the four modes of SYMFONY_HTTP_RECORDER.</p>
        <p>[click] Empty, or replay: we replay the file. Missing entry: exception, never the network. It's the default, and it's what runs in CI.</p>
        <p>[click] missing: we replay what exists, and if an entry is missing, we make the real call and append it. That's what we use for a first recording, or when we add a new call to an existing test.</p>
        <p>[click] record: we always make the real call, and rewrite the files of the tests that ran. That's for refreshing the recordings after an API change.</p>
        <p>[click] And passthrough: real calls, without reading or writing the files. Useful to check now and then that your tests still pass against the real services.</p>
        <p>[click] The principle behind all this has a name: fail closed. A committed test never touches the network. Recording is always an explicit choice, made at launch.</p>
        <p>Why does it matter so much? Imagine the default were to record missing entries. You rename a URL in a test, the entry is no longer found… and the test silently calls the real service. In CI. With the consequences you can imagine if it's a payment API.</p>
    </aside>
</section>
